<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The boat's Signal K server. It lives on the vessel's own LAN, on an address the crew types or
  the app finds over mDNS, and it is almost always plain HTTP: a boat server has no public name
  and no certificate anybody can issue for 192.168.1.9. Since the host cannot be known in advance
  it cannot be listed here, so cleartext is permitted.

  What that is worth knowing about, and why it is bounded: this is the app's ONLY HTTP traffic.
  The channel itself never uses it — every packet between phones is AES-256-GCM under the channel
  key over UDP, Bluetooth RFCOMM or Wi-Fi Aware, and none of it goes through this stack. What
  crosses in the clear is a Signal K read, an announcement, and the token that authorises them,
  all on the boat's own network. docs/SECURITY.md says so in the threat model.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
